C# 14: The field Keyword — Cleaner Properties, Zero Boilerplate
Every C# developer has been there. You start with a clean auto-property, then requirements change and you need to add a tiny bit of validation. Suddenly that one-liner explodes into six lines of boilerplate — a private backing field, a getter that just returns it, a setter that assigns it. The logic is two words. The ceremony is everything else. C# 14 fixes this with the field keyword: a contextual keyword that refers to the compiler-synthesized backing field of a property, letting you write custom accessor logic without ever declaring an explicit field. Auto-properties are one of C#'s best quality-of-life features. This is clean: public string Username { get; set; }
But the moment you need to trim whitespace on assignment,…
